// main.vix — the cargo TOOL package, the provider side of the seam
// (../facet is the consumer side).
//
// THIS PACKAGE IS THE CORPUS ENTRY THAT CURRENTLY CANNOT BE WRITTEN IN FULL,
// and that is why it is kept. It exposes `rustc` and `cargo` as capabilities,
// sourced from a fetched toolchain distribution — and after
// r[vixen.package.inputs-are-provider-pins] was struck, a program sourced
// from OUTSIDE this package has no spelling. The `@input` arm was that
// spelling; it went with the retraction.
//
// What the manifest below can say is therefore only half of what this package
// is. The missing half is a named gap, not an omission: see the Retracted
// section of /spec/vixen/packages. A toolchain a package did not build is
// *acquirable* — pinned, served at a VFS prefix, identity known a priori from
// the pin — and how a manifest names one is undesigned.
//
// The retraction was right even though it broke this file. Arbitrary vix code
// can fetch anything at any time as an ordinary demand; a declarative list of
// sources fixed up front was a Nix reflex. But the OTHER question the struck
// section was answering — when a fn demands `Rustc`, which package satisfies
// it, at which identity — is real, is configuration rather than code, and now
// has no home. This file is the standing evidence of that.

manifest """
package {
  name cargo
  version "0.1.0"
}

// `vx r cargo build` in a bare cargo workspace — no main.vix anywhere in
// sight — resolves THIS package through the executor's default registry pin,
// and `build` reads the cwd's Cargo.toml and Cargo.lock as data, the lock's
// checksums being the fetch pins (vixen.pins.come-from-the-ecosystem-
// lockfile). The no-config cargo build is this line. `fn cargo::build` names
// the orchestrator that corpus-next/cargo.vix and the demo-1 mission are
// converging on: manifest ingestion → rodin solve → fetch by cksum →
// extract → build walk → real rustc.
command {
  build fn>cargo::build
  test fn>cargo::test
}

// A `program` section belongs here and cannot be written. It would expose:
//
//   rustc — rustc/bin/rustc from a pinned rust-dist archive,
//           protocol @exit-only, target @argv-flag{ flag "--target" }
//   cargo — cargo/bin/cargo from the same archive, exposed for oracle runs
//           only; the commands above never exec it, `build` IS cargo
//           reimplemented in vix
//
// Both need a source arm naming something this package did not build. The
// surviving arm is @artifact, which names one of this package's own outputs.
// Neither of these is that.
"""

// The orchestrator. Where the toolchain comes from is this fn's business,
// discharged as ordinary demands — which is exactly the point the retraction
// made, and exactly why the manifest above is shorter than it used to be.
fn build(rustc: Rustc, sh: Sh) -> Tree {
    ...
}

fn test(rustc: Rustc, sh: Sh) -> Stream<Check> {
    ...
}
